uid "62662e92-d5bd-5319-9c3c-2bad4d39f6bc" 13194139535914 true] [17592186374113 1017 #edb/ref 17592186374114 13194139535914 true] [17592186374113 1017 #edb/ref 17592186374120 13194139535914 true] [17592186374113 1089 "q-340675" 13194139535914 true] [17592186374113 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535914 true] [17592186374113 1100 "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139535914 true] [17592186374113 1113 #uuid "29f219e9-01ff-5c63-a4b3-4b540c752b19" 13194139535914 true] [17592186374113 1145 "We begin by using the half-angle identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡(6x)=\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)+\\frac{1}{24}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(24\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ $\\text{are not orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139535914 true] [17592186374114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374115 13194139535914 true] [17592186374114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374116 13194139535914 true] [17592186374114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374117 13194139535914 true] [17592186374114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374118 13194139535914 true] [17592186374114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374119 13194139535914 true] [17592186374114 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535914 true